  "cards": [
    {"dot": "cyan", "title": "SSH on TCP 2222 is an illustration", "items": ["A registered port name does not establish the actual protocol. A clear SSH identification string supports service identification even on a nonstandard port (docs/deep-packet-inspection.md:14-23).", "These are cooperating identification methods, not four required serial stages. Greeting parsers and stream decoders remain useful with -dpi=false (docs/deep-packet-inspection.md:19-34)."]},
    {"dot": "emerald", "title": "DPI has explicit runtime limits", "items": ["Native classification requires a DPI build and initialization. GetProtocols returns nil while disabled (internal/dpi/dpi.go:156-172).", "The incremental pool uses scoped flow keys, bounded inspection, idle expiry and capacity eviction. Defaults are up to 8 workers, 65,536 flows and a 5-minute idle timeout (incremental.go:28-67,149-170,234-303).", "A TLS or QUIC application label does not expose the HTTP plaintext inside it (docs/deep-packet-inspection.md:34)."]},
    {"dot": "amber", "title": "A banner is an advertised identity", "items": ["Ambiguous greetings, missing bytes and encryption can withhold identification. A server can advertise software it does not run (docs/deep-packet-inspection.md:44-50).", "Next: software.html follows endpoint attribution; vulnerabilities.html follows version applicability. Both start from the evidence actually observed."]}
  ]
